meta {
  name: ListOmschrijvingEncodedAmpersand
  type: http
  seq: 19
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/web/artikel?omschrijving=A%26B&pageSize=5
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  X-Auth-Token: {{authToken}}
  Accept: application/json
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.pageSize: eq 5
}

tests {
  test("responds in well under the 11.8-19.8s latency previously reproduced for this exact input", function() {
    expect(res.getResponseTime()).to.be.below(5000);
  });
}

docs {
  Regression test for a percent-encoded "&" INSIDE a filter value:
  omschrijving=A%26B decodes (once, by the framework) to the literal
  value "A&B" by the time Luna.Web.Util.QueryParamHelper reads it off
  URI:QueryString. The old blind-split-on-"&" implementation mis-split
  this into "omschrijving=A" plus a dangling "B" piece, silently
  truncating the filter to "A" - functionally wrong (matched far more
  rows than the real "A&B" value would) though not slow, since "A" is a
  common substring. Fixed by anchoring pair-splitting on the known
  query-param name set (see the QueryParamHelper class header) instead
  of blindly splitting on every "&".

  This does not by itself reproduce the 11.8-19.8s latency bug reported
  against ?omschrijving=A%3DB&pageSize=5 (see
  ListOmschrijvingEncodedEquals.bru) - that lives in
  Luna.BusinessLogic.ArtikelBE's full-table substring scan, not in this
  parsing layer, and is now capped independently (iMaxRowsScanned in
  ArtikelBE:GetArtikelen). The timing assertion here is a generous
  regression guard against either bug reappearing, not a tight
  performance benchmark - see ListOmschrijvingEncodedEquals.bru for the
  scenario that actually reproduced the multi-second latency.
}